
# Problem Statement

## 1. Background

Waste management and recycling are important for reducing the amount of waste sent to landfills and encouraging more responsible disposal practices. However, people may be unsure how to classify certain waste items or where to send them for recycling. This can make recycling less convenient, especially when users do not know which facilities accept particular types of waste.

## 2. Problem Description

The main problem addressed by this project is the difficulty of identifying waste categories and finding suitable nearby recycling facilities through a single, convenient application. Users may have to identify waste materials themselves and search for recycling information from different sources. Incorrect classification or a lack of information about nearby facilities may discourage users from recycling properly. Therefore, a mobile application that combines image-based waste classification with location-based recycling facility information could help users make more informed disposal decisions.

## 3. Target Users and Stakeholders

The proposed system is intended for:

- **General public:** People who want to identify waste items and find suitable recycling facilities.
- **Students and households:** People who want to improve their daily recycling practices.
- **Recycling facility operators:** Facilities that may benefit from users finding their locations and learning what materials they accept.
- **Environmental organisations and local authorities:** Stakeholders interested in promoting recycling and responsible waste disposal.

## 4. Supporting Evidence

- **Source title:** AI-Powered Waste Classification Using Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs)
- **Author or organisation:** Chan Jia Yi and Chong Fong Kim
- **Publication year:** 2024
- **Relevant finding:** The study reports that around 72.19% of residents surveyed were unsure about waste categorisation and proper waste disposal. It explores CNN-based waste classification as a way to address this issue. This supports the need for a system that helps users identify waste correctly.
- **Source link:** https://doi.org/10.14569/IJACSA.2024.0151009

## 5. Proposed Solution

This project proposes an AI-based mobile application that uses a Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) to classify waste from an image captured or uploaded by a user. The application will display the predicted waste category and use geolocation to help users find nearby recycling facilities from an available facility database. The proposed solution aims to make waste identification and recycling facility discovery more convenient.

## 6. Project Scope

### Included

- Capturing or uploading waste images
- CNN-based waste image classification
- Displaying predicted waste categories
- Obtaining the user's location after permission is granted
- Finding nearby recycling facilities using location data
- Displaying available facility details, such as name, address, location, and accepted materials where available

### Excluded

- Physical waste collection
- Managing recycling facility operations
- Online payments
- Real-time tracking of waste collection vehicles
- Guaranteeing that every listed facility is open or accepts every type of waste

## 7. Expected Outcome

The expected outcome is a mobile application prototype that demonstrates waste image classification and location-based recycling facility discovery. Its classification performance and the accuracy of its facility information will need to be evaluated during development and testing.
